Ansible-core 2.19 introduced stricter Jinja sandbox restrictions that block access to underscored attributes and collection mutation methods. This breaks existing playbooks that use patterns like `{% set result = [] %}` and `{% set _ = result.append(key) %}` to work around Jinja language limitations. Users need an opt-in compatibility mode to allow these patterns temporarily while they rewrite their playbooks. result append data key test foo bar data:
  foo: value1
  bar: value2

test: |-
  {% set result = [] %}
  {% for key in data %}
  {% if data[key].endswith('2') %}
  {% set _ = result.append(key) %}
  {% endif %}
  {% endfor %}
  {{ result }}

